WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.063 --> 00:00:03.328
With every change comes loss.

2
00:00:03.328 --> 00:00:04.960
I struggle to motivate myself sometimes.

3
00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:06.880
But maybe I have to go 'cause,

4
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:08.072
I don't know what else there is the.

5
00:00:08.072 --> 00:00:10.409
How the fuck did I end up in this situation?

6
00:00:10.409 --> 00:00:11.488
Depression.

7
00:00:11.488 --> 00:00:13.138
I can't concentrate at work.

8
00:00:13.138 --> 00:00:13.971
And grief.

9
00:00:13.971 --> 00:00:15.514
Screaming at the top of my lungs.

10
00:00:15.514 --> 00:00:16.515
And sadness.

11
00:00:16.515 --> 00:00:17.490
Screw you, I don't want any help,

12
00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:19.323
I'm gonna kill myself.

13
00:00:34.365 --> 00:00:37.621
After eight plus years and over 600 conversations,

14
00:00:37.621 --> 00:00:41.690
I've compiled a fairly massive library of practical

15
00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:45.420
information and timeless wisdom that

16
00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:47.268
due to the nature of the internet

17
00:00:47.268 --> 00:00:50.414
often gets lost in the seemingly endless feed

18
00:00:50.414 --> 00:00:53.780
of new content released every single day.

19
00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:56.849
And so I felt compelled to experiment with

20
00:00:56.849 --> 00:00:58.488
a new episode format.

21
00:00:58.488 --> 00:01:01.662
One that features a compilation of some of the best guests

22
00:01:01.662 --> 00:01:03.537
I've had on the show in the past,

23
00:01:03.537 --> 00:01:07.562
and centers around a single theme or subject matter.

24
00:01:07.562 --> 00:01:09.774
This is the second deep dive.

25
00:01:09.774 --> 00:01:12.567
The second masterclass episode we've done.

26
00:01:12.567 --> 00:01:15.168
Part one about the microbiome was released back

27
00:01:15.168 --> 00:01:16.001
in January.

28
00:01:16.001 --> 00:01:17.073
And if you missed it,

29
00:01:17.073 --> 00:01:18.441
you can find a link to that episode

30
00:01:18.441 --> 00:01:20.619
in the description below.

31
00:01:20.619 --> 00:01:24.376
But today, we're continuing the experiment with an extremely

32
00:01:24.376 --> 00:01:26.006
important subject,

33
00:01:26.006 --> 00:01:27.240
mental health.

34
00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:30.152
All of the guests featured in this episode are either

35
00:01:30.152 --> 00:01:33.173
experts in one of the various mental health fields,

36
00:01:33.173 --> 00:01:36.212
or are individuals who have gleaned wisdom about

37
00:01:36.212 --> 00:01:38.582
mental health through struggle.

38
00:01:38.582 --> 00:01:40.474
These are truly some of the best,

39
00:01:40.474 --> 00:01:44.292
most inspiring conversations I've had on this topic.

40
00:01:44.292 --> 00:01:46.860
And you can find the full episodes for each in the

41
00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:49.020
description below.

42
00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:50.110
For too long,

43
00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:53.829
talking about mental health has been a social taboo.

44
00:01:53.829 --> 00:01:57.558
Many people have historically been too ashamed to admit they

45
00:01:57.558 --> 00:01:59.362
need help and to seek it out.

46
00:01:59.362 --> 00:02:02.079
And our society's lack of mental health awareness

47
00:02:02.079 --> 00:02:06.113
and education has led to an epidemic of chronic stress

48
00:02:06.113 --> 00:02:08.327
and severe loneliness,

49
00:02:08.327 --> 00:02:10.494
as well as arise and things like suicide,

50
00:02:10.494 --> 00:02:12.161
anxiety, depression,

51
00:02:13.376 --> 00:02:16.862
and Alzheimer's disease and other forms of dementia.

52
00:02:16.862 --> 00:02:19.829
And it is thus vital that we talk about it.

53
00:02:19.829 --> 00:02:22.075
So to better understand mental health,

54
00:02:22.075 --> 00:02:25.255
we begin today's show by talking about therapy

55
00:02:25.255 --> 00:02:27.115
with Lori Gottlieb.

56
00:02:27.115 --> 00:02:29.250
Lori is a Los Angeles based psychotherapist.

57
00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:30.983
She's a journalist,

58
00:02:30.983 --> 00:02:32.361
she's an author of the book

59
00:02:32.361 --> 00:02:34.371
"Maybe You Should Talk to Someone."

60
00:02:34.371 --> 00:02:38.706
And our conversation was about what happens when a therapist

61
00:02:38.706 --> 00:02:42.453
experiences her own personal crisis and seeks help.

62
00:02:42.453 --> 00:02:45.201
But it's also a broader conversation about mental wellbeing

63
00:02:45.201 --> 00:02:46.034
in general,

64
00:02:46.034 --> 00:02:49.079
which makes it the perfect place to start.

65
00:02:49.079 --> 00:02:52.330
(bright music)

66
00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:54.837
Do you think everybody should be in therapy?

67
00:02:54.837 --> 00:02:56.311 line:15%
Since the book came out,

68
00:02:56.311 --> 00:02:57.769 line:15%
everybody asks me that and--

69
00:02:57.769 --> 00:02:58.602
Do they?

70
00:02:58.602 --> 00:02:59.435 line:15%
They do.

71
00:02:59.435 --> 00:03:00.692 line:15%
And I think it's interesting.

72
00:03:00.692 --> 00:03:02.066 line:15%
I'm not, you know,

73
00:03:02.066 --> 00:03:04.349 line:15%
I think everybody can benefit from therapy.

74
00:03:04.349 --> 00:03:08.065
I don't think everybody needs to be in therapy.

75
00:03:08.065 --> 00:03:09.354
But I do think this,

76
00:03:09.354 --> 00:03:12.946
I think that so many times people come to therapy later than

77
00:03:12.946 --> 00:03:13.988
they should,

78
00:03:13.988 --> 00:03:17.902
meaning that we think about our physical wellbeing

79
00:03:17.902 --> 00:03:19.277
different from our emotional wellbeing.

80
00:03:19.277 --> 00:03:21.452
So if somebody feels like something is wrong

81
00:03:21.452 --> 00:03:22.285
with their body,

82
00:03:22.285 --> 00:03:23.323
like you're having chest pain,

83
00:03:23.323 --> 00:03:25.784
you'll probably go to the cardiologist before you have a

84
00:03:25.784 --> 00:03:27.083
massive heart attack.

85
00:03:27.083 --> 00:03:28.041
But if somebody feels like,

86
00:03:28.041 --> 00:03:30.412
oh, something doesn't feel right emotionally,

87
00:03:30.412 --> 00:03:32.670
we tend to ignore it or minimize it.

88
00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:35.626
"Well, I have a roof over my head and food on the table

89
00:03:35.626 --> 00:03:37.492
so I don't really need help."

90
00:03:37.492 --> 00:03:38.987
And what happens is,

91
00:03:38.987 --> 00:03:40.351
they think that they can just make the

92
00:03:40.351 --> 00:03:42.395
feelings go away or not pay attention to them.

93
00:03:42.395 --> 00:03:44.760
But when you don't pay attention to feelings,

94
00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:46.261
they become stronger.

95
00:03:46.261 --> 00:03:48.476
And they come out in a behavior,

96
00:03:48.476 --> 00:03:50.843
in a irritability or in a short temperedness

97
00:03:50.843 --> 00:03:53.010
or in a self-defeating,

98
00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:55.981
self-sabotaging way of being in the world, whatever it is,

99
00:03:55.981 --> 00:03:58.649
or in an inability to sleep, whatever.

100
00:03:58.649 --> 00:04:02.522
And so people then don't come to therapy until they're

101
00:04:02.522 --> 00:04:05.060
having some kind of crisis.

102
00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:07.569
And it's like, they're having an emotional heart attack.

103
00:04:07.569 --> 00:04:09.402
I mean, there's no downside?

104
00:04:09.402 --> 00:04:11.069
Well, time, money.

105
00:04:12.996 --> 00:04:15.374
The biggest downside I think for people,

106
00:04:15.374 --> 00:04:17.198
even though there are logistical problems

107
00:04:17.198 --> 00:04:18.489
that are very real,

108
00:04:18.489 --> 00:04:22.354
is that a lot of people kind of unconsciously know that if

109
00:04:22.354 --> 00:04:23.270
they go to therapy,

110
00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:25.222
they might need to make changes.

111
00:04:25.222 --> 00:04:26.171
Right, yeah.

112
00:04:26.171 --> 00:04:27.004
You know it's like

113
00:04:27.004 --> 00:04:28.594
if I go to therapy.
who wants that?

114
00:04:28.594 --> 00:04:30.796
Like I might need to do something different in my life.

115
00:04:30.796 --> 00:04:33.328
I might need to be uncomfortable

116
00:04:33.328 --> 00:04:34.821
and I don't wanna be uncomfortable.

117
00:04:34.821 --> 00:04:36.234
So I'd rather just, you know,

118
00:04:36.234 --> 00:04:39.061
just keep things the way they are,

119
00:04:39.061 --> 00:04:40.056
status quo.

120
00:04:40.056 --> 00:04:41.179
In the book I say,

121
00:04:41.179 --> 00:04:43.302
"Insight is the booby prize of therapy,"

122
00:04:43.302 --> 00:04:46.380
because you can have all the insight in the world like,

123
00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:48.504
"Oh, now I understand why I keep picking those guys."

124
00:04:48.504 --> 00:04:51.284
But if you don't make changes out in the world,

125
00:04:51.284 --> 00:04:54.595
i.e. pick other guys, the insight is useless.

126
00:04:54.595 --> 00:04:56.223
So many times people in couples therapy will say like,

127
00:04:56.223 --> 00:05:00.788
"Oh, now I understand why I get really triggered by that,

128
00:05:00.788 --> 00:05:01.895
when you do that."

129
00:05:01.895 --> 00:05:03.734
You know, or, "And why I react that way."

130
00:05:03.734 --> 00:05:05.449
But then they don't change anything.

131
00:05:05.449 --> 00:05:06.769
"Well, you're wasting your time

132
00:05:06.769 --> 00:05:08.045
if you're not gonna change anything.

133
00:05:08.045 --> 00:05:09.178
So I think in therapy,

134
00:05:09.178 --> 00:05:11.543
you have to be both vulnerable and accountable.

135
00:05:11.543 --> 00:05:12.709
So you have to be vulnerable,

136
00:05:12.709 --> 00:05:14.685
but you also have to be accountable for

137
00:05:14.685 --> 00:05:17.266
what are you gonna do with the work that we're doing?

138
00:05:17.266 --> 00:05:20.852
How are you gonna make some tangible changes in your

139
00:05:20.852 --> 00:05:22.876
behavior when you leave here?

140
00:05:22.876 --> 00:05:24.327
The change part is so hard.

141
00:05:24.327 --> 00:05:26.175
Right, and people don't realize that they think,

142
00:05:26.175 --> 00:05:27.529
"Oh, it's positive change.

143
00:05:27.529 --> 00:05:29.025
So it should be easy because people want

144
00:05:29.025 --> 00:05:31.885
something better to happen."

145
00:05:31.885 --> 00:05:34.834
But with every change comes loss.

146
00:05:34.834 --> 00:05:37.187
Yeah, the revelation feels like progress,

147
00:05:37.187 --> 00:05:38.606
but it's actually not doing anything.

148
00:05:38.606 --> 00:05:40.594
I mean, I've been sober a long time and you know,

149
00:05:40.594 --> 00:05:42.126
the adage in 12-Step is

150
00:05:42.126 --> 00:05:44.191
"Self knowledge will avail you nothing."

151
00:05:44.191 --> 00:05:45.124
(Lori laughs)
Right!

152
00:05:45.124 --> 00:05:46.288
And when people say to me like,

153
00:05:46.288 --> 00:05:47.833
"Well, why do you think you were an alcoholic?

154
00:05:47.833 --> 00:05:48.789
Like what happened when you're,"

155
00:05:48.789 --> 00:05:50.741
And I've learned,

156
00:05:50.741 --> 00:05:52.740
like it's interesting to explore that,

157
00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:56.811
but I could spend all of my time trying to deconstruct that.

158
00:05:56.811 --> 00:05:59.058
And it actually doesn't help, you know,

159
00:05:59.058 --> 00:06:01.509
it doesn't inform how I'm gonna make decisions

160
00:06:01.509 --> 00:06:02.714
going forward.

161
00:06:02.714 --> 00:06:06.162
I have this toolbox now and I apply these tools to how I

162
00:06:06.162 --> 00:06:09.382
make decisions and how I run things by other people that

163
00:06:09.382 --> 00:06:12.673
have allowed me to maintain my sobriety and

164
00:06:12.673 --> 00:06:16.139
become a more productive member of society.

165
00:06:16.139 --> 00:06:19.588
So for me, it's about like the actionable tools.

166
00:06:19.588 --> 00:06:21.954
And I think it is like,

167
00:06:21.954 --> 00:06:25.321
it's so difficult for people to change.

168
00:06:25.321 --> 00:06:27.108
And in my experience, in my own personal experience,

169
00:06:27.108 --> 00:06:28.626
like I didn't change until I was in,

170
00:06:28.626 --> 00:06:33.459
you know the pain of continuing on the path that I was on,

171
00:06:34.587 --> 00:06:39.147
exceeded the fear of the unknown should I change.

172
00:06:39.147 --> 00:06:40.959
So what is that like?

173
00:06:40.959 --> 00:06:45.256
Like how do you get people short of having to have some,

174
00:06:45.256 --> 00:06:48.510
cataclysm in their life to make those adjustments and

175
00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:51.931
actually put your information and your insight

176
00:06:51.931 --> 00:06:53.598
into forward motion.

177
00:06:55.113 --> 00:06:57.024
There was something that my therapist said to me that I

178
00:06:57.024 --> 00:06:59.064
write about in the book where he said, you know,

179
00:06:59.064 --> 00:07:00.576
where I was like,

180
00:07:00.576 --> 00:07:02.783
just feeling trapped by all of these sort of

181
00:07:02.783 --> 00:07:03.930
external circumstances.

182
00:07:03.930 --> 00:07:05.683
And I wasn't willing to make changes.

183
00:07:05.683 --> 00:07:08.622
I wasn't willing to look at what I could do differently.

184
00:07:08.622 --> 00:07:11.642
And he said, "You remind me of this cartoon.

185
00:07:11.642 --> 00:07:12.747
And it's of this prisoner,

186
00:07:12.747 --> 00:07:15.669
shaking the bars desperately trying to get out,

187
00:07:15.669 --> 00:07:17.992
but on the right and the left, the bars are open."

188
00:07:17.992 --> 00:07:20.114
And it was like, you know,

189
00:07:20.114 --> 00:07:20.947
at first I thought,

190
00:07:20.947 --> 00:07:21.780
"Oh, that's really cheesy."

191
00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:22.678
But then I thought,

192
00:07:22.678 --> 00:07:24.185
"Wait a minute, he's right."

193
00:07:24.185 --> 00:07:26.437
Because so many of us would rather be the prisoners,

194
00:07:26.437 --> 00:07:27.788
shaking the bars saying,

195
00:07:27.788 --> 00:07:30.066
"I can't do anything about this."

196
00:07:30.066 --> 00:07:31.828
We don't wanna look that it's open

197
00:07:31.828 --> 00:07:32.661
on the right or the left.

198
00:07:32.661 --> 00:07:35.735
'Cause then if we walk out and we go into the sunlight and

199
00:07:35.735 --> 00:07:37.703
there are no bars and we're free,

200
00:07:37.703 --> 00:07:39.542
with freedom comes responsibility.

201
00:07:39.542 --> 00:07:41.708
And now we have to take responsibility for our lives.

202
00:07:41.708 --> 00:07:43.585
We can't say, "Oh, the problem is, you know,

203
00:07:43.585 --> 00:07:45.093
that I'm trapped here."

204
00:07:45.093 --> 00:07:46.364
Now it's like,

205
00:07:46.364 --> 00:07:49.559
anything that happens is I'm responsible for that.

206
00:07:49.559 --> 00:07:52.743
And so change is hard for that reason.

207
00:07:52.743 --> 00:07:55.926
You know, it's easier to feel trapped in the

208
00:07:55.926 --> 00:07:59.801
whatever childhood drama that you're reenacting

209
00:07:59.801 --> 00:08:03.234
than to actually do something different

210
00:08:03.234 --> 00:08:07.257
where you might have to be responsible for your choices.

211
00:08:07.257 --> 00:08:09.840
(bright music)

212
00:08:10.717 --> 00:08:12.477
Taking responsibility for your mental health

213
00:08:12.477 --> 00:08:14.477
is absolutely key.

214
00:08:14.477 --> 00:08:16.747
And it can be a hard pill to swallow for the first time.

215
00:08:16.747 --> 00:08:20.607
But ultimately it's really the only way forward if you wanna

216
00:08:20.607 --> 00:08:23.340
make positive change in your life.

217
00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:27.197
But what do we do when we just can't find the strength

218
00:08:27.197 --> 00:08:29.607
within ourselves to work past our fear,

219
00:08:29.607 --> 00:08:33.037
take responsibility for our failures and seek that help.

220
00:08:33.037 --> 00:08:37.207
I mean, mental health disorders can literally impede our

221
00:08:37.207 --> 00:08:40.827
decision-making processes just by nature of what they are

222
00:08:40.827 --> 00:08:42.747
because they deal with the mind.

223
00:08:42.747 --> 00:08:45.987
And how do you solve a problem of the mind when that same

224
00:08:45.987 --> 00:08:48.687
mind created the problem in the first place?

225
00:08:48.687 --> 00:08:49.990
Well, this next clip,

226
00:08:49.990 --> 00:08:51.487
a section from my conversation with

227
00:08:51.487 --> 00:08:54.037
author and journalist, Johann Hari,

228
00:08:54.037 --> 00:08:56.637
addresses that very question.

229
00:08:56.637 --> 00:08:58.417
Johann has written for the New York Times,

230
00:08:58.417 --> 00:08:59.925 line:15%
the Los Angeles Times,

231
00:08:59.925 --> 00:09:01.316 line:15%
and many other outlets.

232
00:09:01.316 --> 00:09:04.381 line:15%
He is also the author of two quite profound books,

233
00:09:04.381 --> 00:09:05.601 line:15%
"Chasing the Scream,"

234
00:09:05.601 --> 00:09:07.874
and his more recent book, "Lost Connections"

235
00:09:07.874 --> 00:09:09.426
which is a quite compelling

236
00:09:09.426 --> 00:09:11.808
deep dive into the nature of depression

237
00:09:11.808 --> 00:09:14.115
and its underlying causes.

238
00:09:14.115 --> 00:09:17.937
My original conversation with Johann was a super interesting

239
00:09:17.937 --> 00:09:20.298
exploration into those causes,

240
00:09:20.298 --> 00:09:22.317
into those reasons,

241
00:09:22.317 --> 00:09:24.483
and the cultural drivers behind both

242
00:09:24.483 --> 00:09:26.337
addiction and depression,

243
00:09:26.337 --> 00:09:28.578
as well as mental health in general.

244
00:09:28.578 --> 00:09:31.607
What contributes to their malignancy,

245
00:09:31.607 --> 00:09:34.297
what compel so many in this unhealthy direction,

246
00:09:34.297 --> 00:09:38.198
and why they are so difficult to overcome.

247
00:09:38.198 --> 00:09:40.397
I'm very excited to share this clip with you because

248
00:09:40.397 --> 00:09:42.288
it moves the way we think about

249
00:09:42.288 --> 00:09:45.230
our mental health journey away from being self-focused.

250
00:09:45.230 --> 00:09:50.230
And instead, places it within the context of community,

251
00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:54.912
as Johann shares the stories of a woman in pain,

252
00:09:54.912 --> 00:09:56.245
a boy with ADHD,

253
00:09:57.080 --> 00:09:58.705
a man in custody,

254
00:09:58.705 --> 00:10:00.525
and the neighbors who saved their lives.

255
00:10:00.525 --> 00:10:03.165
(bright music)

256
00:10:03.165 --> 00:10:06.406 line:15%
In the summer of 2011 on a big anonymous housing project

257
00:10:06.406 --> 00:10:07.239 line:15%
in Berlin,

258
00:10:08.151 --> 00:10:11.973 line:15%
a woman called Nuriye Cengiz climbed out of her wheelchair

259
00:10:11.973 --> 00:10:13.290 line:15%
and she put a sign in her window.

260
00:10:13.290 --> 00:10:15.701
She lived in a ground floor apartment.

261
00:10:15.701 --> 00:10:17.905
And the sign said something like,

262
00:10:17.905 --> 00:10:20.478
"I got a notice saying I'm going to be evicted

263
00:10:20.478 --> 00:10:22.237
from my apartment next Friday.

264
00:10:22.237 --> 00:10:25.568
So next Thursday night, I'm going to kill myself."

265
00:10:25.568 --> 00:10:27.003
No one knew this woman.

266
00:10:27.003 --> 00:10:29.626
People start to knock on her door and they're like,

267
00:10:29.626 --> 00:10:30.888
"Do you need any help?"

268
00:10:30.888 --> 00:10:32.199
She said, "Screw you I don't want any help,

269
00:10:32.199 --> 00:10:33.300
I'm gonna kill myself."

270
00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:35.840
(somber music)

271
00:10:35.840 --> 00:10:37.741
And outside the apartment,

272
00:10:37.741 --> 00:10:38.574
some of the people who lived there,

273
00:10:38.574 --> 00:10:40.570
they didn't know each other started chatting.

274
00:10:40.570 --> 00:10:42.486
And one of them had this idea.

275
00:10:42.486 --> 00:10:44.937
There's a big thoroughfare that goes into the central Berlin

276
00:10:44.937 --> 00:10:45.961
that runs through this project.

277
00:10:45.961 --> 00:10:47.589
It's called Kotti.

278
00:10:47.589 --> 00:10:48.943
One of them had an idea,

279
00:10:48.943 --> 00:10:53.360
:If we just block the street for a day on a Saturday,

280
00:10:54.214 --> 00:10:55.414
there probably be a fuss,

281
00:10:55.414 --> 00:10:56.884
the media will probably come,

282
00:10:56.884 --> 00:10:58.834
they'll probably let Nuriye stay in her apartment.

283
00:10:58.834 --> 00:11:01.174
Maybe there'll be some pressure for us to,

284
00:11:01.174 --> 00:11:02.007
you know,

285
00:11:02.007 --> 00:11:05.653
our rents to be kept down too and this disaster would stop."

286
00:11:05.653 --> 00:11:06.653
And so they decided to do it.

287
00:11:06.653 --> 00:11:09.953
They went and built a barricade in the middle of the road

288
00:11:09.953 --> 00:11:10.786
and Nuriye was like,

289
00:11:10.786 --> 00:11:11.729
"Well, I'm gonna kill myself anyway.

290
00:11:11.729 --> 00:11:12.758
I might as well let them wheel me out of there."

291
00:11:12.758 --> 00:11:13.782
It doesn't matter.

292
00:11:13.782 --> 00:11:16.442
They wheel her out there and the media did come and they

293
00:11:16.442 --> 00:11:18.722
interviewed Nuriye and the other residents,

294
00:11:18.722 --> 00:11:20.212
a bit of fuss and

295
00:11:20.212 --> 00:11:21.742
it gets to the end of the day and the police say,

296
00:11:21.742 --> 00:11:24.082
"Okay, you've had your fun, take it down."

297
00:11:24.082 --> 00:11:25.242
But the people that lived in Kotti said,

298
00:11:25.242 --> 00:11:26.641
"Well, hang on a minute.

299
00:11:26.641 --> 00:11:28.964
You haven't told Nuriye if she gets to stay.

300
00:11:28.964 --> 00:11:31.447
And actually we want a rent freeze for our entire block.

301
00:11:31.447 --> 00:11:32.759
It's this whole housing project.

302
00:11:32.759 --> 00:11:34.823
So when you give us that,

303
00:11:34.823 --> 00:11:36.903
we'll take it down."

304
00:11:36.903 --> 00:11:38.336
But of course they knew the minute they left the barricade,

305
00:11:38.336 --> 00:11:39.802
the police would just tear it down.

306
00:11:39.802 --> 00:11:43.148
So people start to sign up to man the barricade, right?

307
00:11:43.148 --> 00:11:44.456
Don't know each other.

308
00:11:44.456 --> 00:11:47.014
Tania, in her tiny little miniskirt

309
00:11:47.014 --> 00:11:50.861
was paired with Nuriye in her full hijab, right?

310
00:11:50.861 --> 00:11:53.885
And these kinds of pairings were happening over CATI.

311
00:11:53.885 --> 00:11:57.997
And first I think they had the Thursday night shift,

312
00:11:57.997 --> 00:11:59.279
Tania and Nuriye are like,

313
00:11:59.279 --> 00:12:00.183
(Hari scoffs)

314
00:12:00.183 --> 00:12:01.683
"We got nothing to talk about, right?

315
00:12:01.683 --> 00:12:02.622
What are we gonna talk about?"

316
00:12:02.622 --> 00:12:04.588
It couldn't be more likely.

317
00:12:04.588 --> 00:12:06.353
So they sit there through the night,

318
00:12:06.353 --> 00:12:08.724
barely speaking first few times.

319
00:12:08.724 --> 00:12:11.946
As the night went on, they started to talk.

320
00:12:11.946 --> 00:12:12.922
They realized that there's something incredibly

321
00:12:12.922 --> 00:12:15.295
powerful and common.

322
00:12:15.295 --> 00:12:19.421
Nuriye told Tania something she'd never told anyone.

323
00:12:19.421 --> 00:12:22.281
So she had come to Berlin when she was 16,

324
00:12:22.281 --> 00:12:25.300
from a village in Turkey with her two young children.

325
00:12:25.300 --> 00:12:28.561
And she was sent to raise enough money to send back for her

326
00:12:28.561 --> 00:12:30.399
husband to come and join her.

327
00:12:30.399 --> 00:12:31.911
After she'd been in Berlin for a year,

328
00:12:31.911 --> 00:12:34.685
she got word from home that her husband had died.

329
00:12:34.685 --> 00:12:37.749
And she told Tania that she'd always told people that he

330
00:12:37.749 --> 00:12:40.230
died of a heart attack.

331
00:12:40.230 --> 00:12:42.313
Actually he died of tuberculosis,

332
00:12:42.313 --> 00:12:44.351
which was seen as like a disease of poverty.

333
00:12:44.351 --> 00:12:46.399
They realized they had both been kind of children with

334
00:12:46.399 --> 00:12:48.446
children themselves in this place

335
00:12:48.446 --> 00:12:50.245
where they'd been very frightened.

336
00:12:50.245 --> 00:12:52.292
They realized that's something really powerful and common,

337
00:12:52.292 --> 00:12:53.641
these kind of pairings were happening all over the Kotti.

338
00:12:53.641 --> 00:12:56.667
There was a young lad called Mehmet who kept being,

339
00:12:56.667 --> 00:12:58.007
they kept nearly throwing him out of school.

340
00:12:58.007 --> 00:12:59.046
They said had ADHD,

341
00:12:59.046 --> 00:13:01.046
was paired with this grumpy old white guy

342
00:13:01.046 --> 00:13:02.707
who said he didn't believe in direct action

343
00:13:02.707 --> 00:13:03.763
'cause he loves style.

344
00:13:03.763 --> 00:13:05.265
And in this case he'd make an exception.

345
00:13:05.265 --> 00:13:06.445
They did a shift together.

346
00:13:06.445 --> 00:13:09.414
The old white guy started helping Mehmet with his homework.

347
00:13:09.414 --> 00:13:11.037
Mehmet started doing a lot better.

348
00:13:11.037 --> 00:13:13.378
As one of the Muslim women there said to me,

349
00:13:13.378 --> 00:13:15.417
"We all realized we had to take these small steps to

350
00:13:15.417 --> 00:13:17.597
understand each other."

351
00:13:17.597 --> 00:13:19.357
After the protest had been going on for about a year,

352
00:13:19.357 --> 00:13:22.129
one day, a man arrived at Kotti.

353
00:13:22.129 --> 00:13:23.016
He is in his early fifties,

354
00:13:23.016 --> 00:13:25.238
he was called Tuncai.

355
00:13:25.238 --> 00:13:26.969
And it's clear when you meet Tuncai

356
00:13:26.969 --> 00:13:28.522
he's got some kind of cognitive difficulties,

357
00:13:28.522 --> 00:13:29.482
he'd been living homeless,

358
00:13:29.482 --> 00:13:31.031
but he also has an amazing energy about him.

359
00:13:31.031 --> 00:13:32.951
Everyone immediately liked him.

360
00:13:32.951 --> 00:13:34.771
And after he was there for two or three days,

361
00:13:34.771 --> 00:13:36.451
that by this time they had actually turned that barricade

362
00:13:36.451 --> 00:13:38.692
into a permanent structure in the street.

363
00:13:38.692 --> 00:13:40.014
And with the roof and everything.

364
00:13:40.014 --> 00:13:40.847
And they said,

365
00:13:40.847 --> 00:13:41.680
"You know, you should start living here.

366
00:13:41.680 --> 00:13:42.513
We don't want you to be homeless."

367
00:13:42.513 --> 00:13:43.506
He started living there.

368
00:13:43.506 --> 00:13:46.737
He became a much loved part of the CATI protest.

369
00:13:46.737 --> 00:13:48.950
Nine months later,

370
00:13:48.950 --> 00:13:50.267
one day the police came to inspect,

371
00:13:50.267 --> 00:13:52.566
they'd to come and inspect every now and then.

372
00:13:52.566 --> 00:13:54.520
And Tuncai doesn't like it when people argue,

373
00:13:54.520 --> 00:13:56.237
he thought the police were arguing.

374
00:13:56.237 --> 00:13:58.488
So he went to try to hug one of the officers.

375
00:13:58.488 --> 00:13:59.795
They thought he was attacking them.

376
00:13:59.795 --> 00:14:01.545
So they arrested him.

377
00:14:02.409 --> 00:14:05.170
That was when it was discovered that Tuncai had been shut

378
00:14:05.170 --> 00:14:08.052
away in a psychiatric hospital

379
00:14:08.052 --> 00:14:10.947
often in a padded cell for 20 years.

380
00:14:10.947 --> 00:14:11.966
He'd escaped one day,

381
00:14:11.966 --> 00:14:12.903
lived on the streets for a few months

382
00:14:12.903 --> 00:14:14.454
and then he found his way to Kotti.

383
00:14:14.454 --> 00:14:17.185
So the police took him back to the psychiatric hospital

384
00:14:17.185 --> 00:14:18.662
and he's put back in his cell,

385
00:14:18.662 --> 00:14:22.037
at which point the entire CATI housing project

386
00:14:22.037 --> 00:14:23.101
turned into a kind of,

387
00:14:23.101 --> 00:14:24.523
"Free Tuncai Movement," right?

388
00:14:24.523 --> 00:14:27.991
They descend on this psychiatric hospital.

389
00:14:27.991 --> 00:14:31.634
I remember Uli canon-bomb one of the protestors

390
00:14:31.634 --> 00:14:32.467
saying to them,

391
00:14:32.467 --> 00:14:34.870
"You know, you don't love him.

392
00:14:34.870 --> 00:14:36.507
He doesn't belong with you.

393
00:14:36.507 --> 00:14:38.367
We love him, he belongs with us."

394
00:14:38.367 --> 00:14:39.367
I remember thinking, you know,

395
00:14:39.367 --> 00:14:41.567
how many of us if someone courted us away,

396
00:14:41.567 --> 00:14:44.485
would have hundreds of people descending saying,

397
00:14:44.485 --> 00:14:46.725
"No, we love this person."

398
00:14:46.725 --> 00:14:49.005
The Bosnian writer Aleksandar Hemon said,

399
00:14:49.005 --> 00:14:51.907
"Home is where people notice when you're there."

400
00:14:51.907 --> 00:14:52.740
Right?

401
00:14:52.740 --> 00:14:55.013
Lots of people are homeless in that sense.

402
00:14:55.013 --> 00:14:58.802
And I remember thinking so clearly in Kotti,

403
00:14:58.802 --> 00:15:00.597
think about how distressed these people were.

404
00:15:00.597 --> 00:15:02.430
Nuriye was about to kill herself.

405
00:15:02.430 --> 00:15:03.798
Mehmet kept being nearly thrown out of school,

406
00:15:03.798 --> 00:15:05.370
they said he had ADHD.

407
00:15:05.370 --> 00:15:07.210
Tuncai was shut away in a padded cell.

408
00:15:07.210 --> 00:15:09.205
Loads of them are depressed and anxious.

409
00:15:09.205 --> 00:15:11.696
These people did not need to be drugged most of them,

410
00:15:11.696 --> 00:15:12.927
they needed to be together.

411
00:15:12.927 --> 00:15:14.882
They needed to have a sense of meaning and purpose.

412
00:15:14.882 --> 00:15:16.551
They needed a home.

413
00:15:16.551 --> 00:15:19.134
(somber music)

414
00:15:20.220 --> 00:15:21.201
I love that.

415
00:15:21.201 --> 00:15:22.034
Once again,

416
00:15:22.034 --> 00:15:24.261
you can find the links to these full conversations in the

417
00:15:24.261 --> 00:15:26.221
description below.

418
00:15:26.221 --> 00:15:30.189
Next up in this mental health deep dive is, Alexi Pappas.

419
00:15:30.189 --> 00:15:31.534
I adore this woman.

420
00:15:31.534 --> 00:15:33.498
She is truly extraordinary.

421
00:15:33.498 --> 00:15:36.134
Not only is Alexi an Olympic athlete,

422
00:15:36.134 --> 00:15:37.561
she's also a poet,

423
00:15:37.561 --> 00:15:40.206
a writer, a filmmaker, and an author.

424
00:15:40.206 --> 00:15:42.169
Her book is called "Bravey"

425
00:15:42.169 --> 00:15:44.173
and it's a beautiful, lovely read.

426
00:15:44.173 --> 00:15:46.256
I highly suggest everybody check it out.

427
00:15:46.256 --> 00:15:50.793
I think most of us consider mental health to be chiefly

428
00:15:50.793 --> 00:15:53.201
an emotional or a chemical issue.

429
00:15:53.201 --> 00:15:55.571
We know mental disorders have to do with something

430
00:15:55.571 --> 00:15:56.469
in our brains,

431
00:15:56.469 --> 00:15:59.118
but we tend to think of them as

432
00:15:59.118 --> 00:16:01.397
thoughts or feelings gone awry

433
00:16:01.397 --> 00:16:04.017
as opposed to something physically broken.

434
00:16:04.017 --> 00:16:08.118
A malady more akin to a scratch or a sprained ankle.

435
00:16:08.118 --> 00:16:09.169
But in this next clip,

436
00:16:09.169 --> 00:16:12.774
Alexi shares her experience of focusing on the physical side

437
00:16:12.774 --> 00:16:13.874
of mental health.

438
00:16:13.874 --> 00:16:16.901
And the way that subtle shift in perspective changed her

439
00:16:16.901 --> 00:16:19.757
life and put her on the path toward healing.

440
00:16:19.757 --> 00:16:22.248
(bright music)

441
00:16:22.248 --> 00:16:25.187
So I was in total denial that I was sick

442
00:16:25.187 --> 00:16:27.854 line:15%
because I didn't understand that

443
00:16:28.763 --> 00:16:31.021 line:15%
your brain can get injured just like your knee.

444
00:16:31.021 --> 00:16:32.534 line:15%
I just, I didn't understand that.

445
00:16:32.534 --> 00:16:37.080 line:15%
And so I was of the mindset as I had always been in my life

446
00:16:37.080 --> 00:16:39.942
that I needed to keep pressing forward

447
00:16:39.942 --> 00:16:41.775
and fix all on my own,

448
00:16:42.681 --> 00:16:45.212
whatever it was that I was feeling to the point where

449
00:16:45.212 --> 00:16:46.725
I wasn't sleeping,

450
00:16:46.725 --> 00:16:49.612
but I tried to force myself to sleep.

451
00:16:49.612 --> 00:16:53.083
And you know, it's called falling asleep for a reason.

452
00:16:53.083 --> 00:16:54.597
You have to let it, right?

453
00:16:54.597 --> 00:16:55.930
Let it happen and--

454
00:16:55.930 --> 00:16:57.598
You being a good type A athlete,

455
00:16:57.598 --> 00:17:00.554
you're trying to know whirl everything into existence.

456
00:17:00.554 --> 00:17:03.939
And then I started to have these even darker thoughts.

457
00:17:03.939 --> 00:17:08.542
And that's when I felt like I understood my mom in a way

458
00:17:08.542 --> 00:17:10.918
that I never wanted to understand her.

459
00:17:10.918 --> 00:17:11.999
Like it's,

460
00:17:11.999 --> 00:17:13.978
you have these thoughts that like,

461
00:17:13.978 --> 00:17:16.086
you wanna die and

462
00:17:16.086 --> 00:17:18.254
I don't think you really want to want to die,

463
00:17:18.254 --> 00:17:20.634
but the thoughts say otherwise,

464
00:17:20.634 --> 00:17:23.340
and that's when you're sick.

465
00:17:23.340 --> 00:17:26.286
And it was terrifying because I always,

466
00:17:26.286 --> 00:17:30.619
I've always been afraid my whole life for the moment

467
00:17:31.762 --> 00:17:33.027
that that might happen to me.

468
00:17:33.027 --> 00:17:35.595
And then when it did,

469
00:17:35.595 --> 00:17:38.563
but before I understood that there was a way to get better,

470
00:17:38.563 --> 00:17:41.640
I thought that that was just my fate.

471
00:17:41.640 --> 00:17:44.500
That like, that was it now happened to me.

472
00:17:44.500 --> 00:17:48.339
And because the narrative I was told about her was,

473
00:17:48.339 --> 00:17:49.519
she just had to go,

474
00:17:49.519 --> 00:17:50.352
like, she just,

475
00:17:50.352 --> 00:17:52.985
she was so sick that she had to go.

476
00:17:52.985 --> 00:17:53.818
And I was like,

477
00:17:53.818 --> 00:17:56.777
"Well, I guess I'm so sick that maybe I have to go

478
00:17:56.777 --> 00:17:58.937
'cause I don't know what else there is.

479
00:17:58.937 --> 00:17:59.795
Wow!

480
00:17:59.795 --> 00:18:02.378
(somber music)

481
00:18:03.231 --> 00:18:05.051
That's so embarrassing honestly,

482
00:18:05.051 --> 00:18:07.761
to share because I don't feel that way anymore,

483
00:18:07.761 --> 00:18:09.461
but I didn't understand.

484
00:18:09.461 --> 00:18:13.421
And I think it's sad that even someone who was susceptible

485
00:18:13.421 --> 00:18:14.707
to these things, you know,

486
00:18:14.707 --> 00:18:18.052
my family history was public, right?

487
00:18:18.052 --> 00:18:20.219
There was no prehab, if you will,

488
00:18:20.219 --> 00:18:21.449
if you wanna call, like,

489
00:18:21.449 --> 00:18:24.644
if we wanna use this body comparison of the brain

490
00:18:24.644 --> 00:18:26.577
is a body part.

491
00:18:26.577 --> 00:18:27.593
I had no prehab.

492
00:18:27.593 --> 00:18:30.159
I had no preparation to deal with this.

493
00:18:30.159 --> 00:18:33.048
And it wasn't until my dad,

494
00:18:33.048 --> 00:18:34.517
because of his experience with my mom,

495
00:18:34.517 --> 00:18:38.680
made me get help that I met a doctor, Doctor Arpaia,

496
00:18:38.680 --> 00:18:43.135
who told me very simply that I was sick and that my brain

497
00:18:43.135 --> 00:18:44.480
had a scratch on it.

498
00:18:44.480 --> 00:18:47.577
And that it could get injured like any other body part,

499
00:18:47.577 --> 00:18:50.230
but it could also heal like any other body part,

500
00:18:50.230 --> 00:18:52.563
and suddenly everything like

501
00:18:52.563 --> 00:18:54.878
it literally turned around in a day.

502
00:18:54.878 --> 00:18:56.148
Not I wasn't happy,

503
00:18:56.148 --> 00:19:00.481
but I believed that I could be on a path to healing,

504
00:19:01.413 --> 00:19:03.304
and that I could commit to it

505
00:19:03.304 --> 00:19:06.364
just like I would in Olympic dream.

506
00:19:06.364 --> 00:19:07.855
We are in a place where we can accept,

507
00:19:07.855 --> 00:19:11.857
I think as a world that elite athletes and high-achievers

508
00:19:11.857 --> 00:19:14.344
can have these mental injuries,

509
00:19:14.344 --> 00:19:16.264
these mental illnesses.

510
00:19:16.264 --> 00:19:19.422
But I think the most important thing now is like,

511
00:19:19.422 --> 00:19:22.060
what do we do about them?

512
00:19:22.060 --> 00:19:24.814
And that was something that I found.

513
00:19:24.814 --> 00:19:27.058
I find that sometimes, you know,

514
00:19:27.058 --> 00:19:29.346
we point fingers at the like,

515
00:19:29.346 --> 00:19:31.634
the pinnacle institutions that we're chasing.

516
00:19:31.634 --> 00:19:35.529
But actually I truly think that this kind of education or

517
00:19:35.529 --> 00:19:37.149
shift has to happen much younger

518
00:19:37.149 --> 00:19:39.649
and on a more universal level,

519
00:19:41.130 --> 00:19:43.569
not just at those pinnacles.

520
00:19:43.569 --> 00:19:45.789
So what would that look like if you were in charge

521
00:19:45.789 --> 00:19:48.099
and could put those things in place?

522
00:19:48.099 --> 00:19:50.349
Well, let's look at body,

523
00:19:52.319 --> 00:19:54.780
the way we've approached the body and

524
00:19:54.780 --> 00:19:57.299
like how that's progressed over the last,

525
00:19:57.299 --> 00:19:58.196
let's say 10 years.

526
00:19:58.196 --> 00:19:59.859
Like 10 years ago,

527
00:19:59.859 --> 00:20:03.589
I don't think my dad or my friend's little sister would have

528
00:20:03.589 --> 00:20:07.756
seen a PT for their body without having an injury,

529
00:20:08.939 --> 00:20:11.639
meaning like regularly take care of their body.

530
00:20:11.639 --> 00:20:13.919
And so just looking at that world,

531
00:20:13.919 --> 00:20:16.927
we've come a long way to accepting that our body is

532
00:20:16.927 --> 00:20:19.729
something not only elite athletes should take care of,

533
00:20:19.729 --> 00:20:21.579
but everybody should take care of,

534
00:20:21.579 --> 00:20:24.269
and that we should take care of it before it's a problem.

535
00:20:24.269 --> 00:20:28.280
Ideally, if you are able to have that kind of support,

536
00:20:28.280 --> 00:20:29.363
and it's not,

537
00:20:30.960 --> 00:20:33.771
there's just like systems and you can get

538
00:20:33.771 --> 00:20:36.251
that kind of help if you can and need it, right?

539
00:20:36.251 --> 00:20:38.753
And I think with mental health,

540
00:20:38.753 --> 00:20:41.920
the comparing it to healing an injury,

541
00:20:44.159 --> 00:20:47.433
is so simple to me and makes so much sense.

542
00:20:47.433 --> 00:20:51.442
So what it would look like to me is accepting honestly

543
00:20:51.442 --> 00:20:54.707
that our brain is a body part and it can get injured.

544
00:20:54.707 --> 00:20:56.747
And when it gets injured,

545
00:20:56.747 --> 00:21:00.383
just like when we break our leg or feel something strange in

546
00:21:00.383 --> 00:21:01.806
our leg,

547
00:21:01.806 --> 00:21:05.819
we have no shame about sharing that something's off and we

548
00:21:05.819 --> 00:21:08.228
get help and we know where to get help.

549
00:21:08.228 --> 00:21:11.388
And it's either built into the system that we're in.

550
00:21:11.388 --> 00:21:13.475
Like a team might have a physio,

551
00:21:13.475 --> 00:21:15.718
they might have a psychologist too.

552
00:21:15.718 --> 00:21:19.254
Or someone can refer you to their favorite physio or their

553
00:21:19.254 --> 00:21:20.087
favorite psycho,

554
00:21:20.087 --> 00:21:22.026
like there's just more accessibility,

555
00:21:22.026 --> 00:21:25.281
just like there is in like the PT world.

556
00:21:25.281 --> 00:21:26.499
And then we get that help.

557
00:21:26.499 --> 00:21:29.960
And we are as kind to ourselves as we are hard on ourselves,

558
00:21:29.960 --> 00:21:32.619
knowing that it's not gonna resolve overnight.

559
00:21:32.619 --> 00:21:35.031
Like nobody is demanding that somebody's broken leg

560
00:21:35.031 --> 00:21:36.359
heal tomorrow.

561
00:21:36.359 --> 00:21:39.355
And so why are we demanding that somebody's depression

562
00:21:39.355 --> 00:21:41.267
heal tomorrow?

563
00:21:41.267 --> 00:21:44.633
Because it's uncomfortable for us to talk about, right?

564
00:21:44.633 --> 00:21:47.552
We wanna pretend that it doesn't exist or there's shame or.

565
00:21:47.552 --> 00:21:49.302
Right, because it's invisible.

566
00:21:49.302 --> 00:21:52.018
But if we like think about it more like an injury,

567
00:21:52.018 --> 00:21:56.475
like no one can see your torn hamstring either,

568
00:21:56.475 --> 00:21:58.303
but they know that it's there if you say it

569
00:21:58.303 --> 00:22:00.426
and they believe it's there.

570
00:22:00.426 --> 00:22:03.040
So if we just see it as more of a physical injury,

571
00:22:03.040 --> 00:22:04.207
which it is,

572
00:22:04.207 --> 00:22:08.205
then I think it becomes a little less subjective

573
00:22:08.205 --> 00:22:10.619
and a little more objective.
Right.

574
00:22:10.619 --> 00:22:12.451
And in the meantime, just being told like,

575
00:22:12.451 --> 00:22:14.287
you're gonna feel this way,

576
00:22:14.287 --> 00:22:17.827
allows you to be in acceptance rather than

577
00:22:17.827 --> 00:22:20.291
beating yourself up because you woke up again

578
00:22:20.291 --> 00:22:21.891
and didn't feel good.

579
00:22:21.891 --> 00:22:24.746
You don't have the secondary emotion of being offended

580
00:22:24.746 --> 00:22:26.690
by the sadness as much.

581
00:22:26.690 --> 00:22:28.090
You almost are like,

582
00:22:28.090 --> 00:22:31.032
you wake up with the sadness like it's, you know,

583
00:22:31.032 --> 00:22:31.956
I don't have a child,

584
00:22:31.956 --> 00:22:32.789
but I think about like,

585
00:22:32.789 --> 00:22:36.532
if a baby's crying and you're at the grocery store,

586
00:22:36.532 --> 00:22:37.365
the baby's crying,

587
00:22:37.365 --> 00:22:39.359
but you still gotta to get the milk, you know?

588
00:22:39.359 --> 00:22:42.469
So I think that in that way sometimes where

589
00:22:42.469 --> 00:22:45.243
you almost have a sense of humor about it,

590
00:22:45.243 --> 00:22:49.405
or at least some levity to understand that it's there.

591
00:22:49.405 --> 00:22:50.780
It will be there for a while.

592
00:22:50.780 --> 00:22:53.763
And you're in the process of making it go away.

593
00:22:53.763 --> 00:22:55.908
Actions change first,

594
00:22:55.908 --> 00:22:58.592
then thoughts, then feelings.

595
00:22:58.592 --> 00:22:59.659
And in that order.

596
00:22:59.659 --> 00:23:03.158
And that was another life saving rule basically,

597
00:23:03.158 --> 00:23:06.370
because what I've observed from my mom was that,

598
00:23:06.370 --> 00:23:09.132
her caretakers were trying to force the feelings

599
00:23:09.132 --> 00:23:13.004
and she was trying to force the feelings to change.

600
00:23:13.004 --> 00:23:14.764
And we can't.

601
00:23:14.764 --> 00:23:17.932
They follow our thoughts, which follow our actions.

602
00:23:17.932 --> 00:23:20.281
Right?
(bright music)

603
00:23:20.281 --> 00:23:23.448
Actions, then thoughts, then feelings.

604
00:23:24.491 --> 00:23:26.692
Practical advice from somebody who has learned

605
00:23:26.692 --> 00:23:28.715
that lesson firsthand.

606
00:23:28.715 --> 00:23:32.279
But what kinds of actions should we take?

607
00:23:32.279 --> 00:23:33.424
Therapy?

608
00:23:33.424 --> 00:23:34.655
Physical therapy?

609
00:23:34.655 --> 00:23:36.415
Reaching out to our neighbors?

610
00:23:36.415 --> 00:23:39.640
And how do we know if those actions are the right ones?

611
00:23:39.640 --> 00:23:41.926
Well, those questions lead directly

612
00:23:41.926 --> 00:23:44.721
to the heart of this next conversation with

613
00:23:44.721 --> 00:23:46.621
Doctor Rangan Chatterjee.

614
00:23:46.621 --> 00:23:47.941
For those unfamiliar,

615
00:23:47.941 --> 00:23:51.117
Doctor Chatterjee is a pioneer in the field of progressive

616
00:23:51.117 --> 00:23:52.239
functional medicine,

617
00:23:52.239 --> 00:23:55.737
widely regarded as one of the most influential doctors

618
00:23:55.737 --> 00:23:56.570
in the UK.

619
00:23:57.722 --> 00:24:01.645
Rangan is double board certified in internal medicine and

620
00:24:01.645 --> 00:24:02.914
family medicine,

621
00:24:02.914 --> 00:24:06.125
and holds an honors degree in immunology.

622
00:24:06.125 --> 00:24:09.270
He also hosts the popular Feel Better Live More Podcast,

623
00:24:09.270 --> 00:24:10.907
which I've appeared on twice,

624
00:24:10.907 --> 00:24:12.723
and has been widely featured on

625
00:24:12.723 --> 00:24:15.583
an array of prominent media outlets like the New York Times,

626
00:24:15.583 --> 00:24:18.271
the BBC, Forbes, The Guardian,

627
00:24:18.271 --> 00:24:20.687
Financial Times, and many others.

628
00:24:20.687 --> 00:24:21.826
In his practice,

629
00:24:21.826 --> 00:24:24.183
Doctor Chatterjee has found himself increasingly

630
00:24:24.183 --> 00:24:26.637
treating patients on the daily who suffer

631
00:24:26.637 --> 00:24:29.219
the downstream implications of

632
00:24:29.219 --> 00:24:30.383
living with chronic stress

633
00:24:30.383 --> 00:24:32.593
and other mental health disorders.

634
00:24:32.593 --> 00:24:34.716
This in turn has given him the opportunity

635
00:24:34.716 --> 00:24:38.045
to treat his patients in unconventional ways,

636
00:24:38.045 --> 00:24:42.114
including a surprising approach you'll hear more right now.

637
00:24:42.114 --> 00:24:44.443
(bright music)

638
00:24:44.443 --> 00:24:46.315
One of the prescriptions I give people

639
00:24:46.315 --> 00:24:48.327 line:15%
and I don't particularly like the term prescription,

640
00:24:48.327 --> 00:24:51.404 line:15%
but one of the recommendations I make is to have a daily

641
00:24:51.404 --> 00:24:54.038 line:15%
dose of pleasure even just for five minutes.

642
00:24:54.038 --> 00:24:57.288
Now, again, just to bring this to life,

643
00:24:58.356 --> 00:25:01.326
I had a patient, 52 year old chap,

644
00:25:01.326 --> 00:25:02.239
or maybe 53,

645
00:25:02.239 --> 00:25:03.471
early fifties chap,

646
00:25:03.471 --> 00:25:06.947
CFO of the local plastics company, right?

647
00:25:06.947 --> 00:25:09.411
Again, comes in to see me.

648
00:25:09.411 --> 00:25:10.244
He's worried,

649
00:25:10.244 --> 00:25:12.514
"Doctor, do I have depression?

650
00:25:12.514 --> 00:25:14.628
What's going on?

651
00:25:14.628 --> 00:25:16.956
I struggle to motivate myself sometimes,

652
00:25:16.956 --> 00:25:18.489
I can't concentrate at work.

653
00:25:18.489 --> 00:25:21.617
I feel quite indifferent about my relationships,

654
00:25:21.617 --> 00:25:22.825
what is going on?"

655
00:25:22.825 --> 00:25:24.148
So as always,

656
00:25:24.148 --> 00:25:26.058
I try to unpack different aspects of their lives.

657
00:25:26.058 --> 00:25:27.388
"Do you like your job?"

658
00:25:27.388 --> 00:25:28.946
"Yeah, not really.

659
00:25:28.946 --> 00:25:30.790
You know, it's so so I have to do it

660
00:25:30.790 --> 00:25:32.121
and I've got a family I need to provide for them."

661
00:25:32.121 --> 00:25:32.954
I said, "Okay, fine,

662
00:25:32.954 --> 00:25:34.784
How's your relationship with your wife?"

663
00:25:34.784 --> 00:25:35.617
(Chatterjee sighs)

664
00:25:35.617 --> 00:25:36.450
"You know what?

665
00:25:36.450 --> 00:25:37.950
So so, you know, I don't really see her that much,

666
00:25:37.950 --> 00:25:38.867
it's fine."

667
00:25:40.240 --> 00:25:42.407
"Do you have any hobbies?"

668
00:25:43.539 --> 00:25:45.077
"Doc, I don't have time for hobbies.

669
00:25:45.077 --> 00:25:45.910
I'm busy.

670
00:25:45.910 --> 00:25:47.033
I can't be late."

671
00:25:47.033 --> 00:25:48.200
I said, "Okay, what about weekends?"

672
00:25:48.200 --> 00:25:50.687
"Weekends, I've got to do the household chores.

673
00:25:50.687 --> 00:25:52.187
I've got to take the kids to their classes.

674
00:25:52.187 --> 00:25:54.385
I don't have time for hobbies."

675
00:25:54.385 --> 00:25:56.968
So the prescription I gave him,

676
00:25:58.094 --> 00:26:01.082
the recommendation I made some of the end of doing this.

677
00:26:01.082 --> 00:26:03.272
And again, so that I don't get, (sighs)

678
00:26:03.272 --> 00:26:04.714
not that it matters if I get criticized,

679
00:26:04.714 --> 00:26:05.936
but just to be one person,

680
00:26:05.936 --> 00:26:08.054
I did do the safety net thing that is required of me.

681
00:26:08.054 --> 00:26:09.780
I didn't feel that he was an active suicide risk.

682
00:26:09.780 --> 00:26:12.106
I didn't do all these sort of various things that I have to

683
00:26:12.106 --> 00:26:12.939
do as a doctor.

684
00:26:12.939 --> 00:26:13.774
I did check.

685
00:26:13.774 --> 00:26:16.706
But ultimately what I felt was that he didn't have any

686
00:26:16.706 --> 00:26:18.625
passion in his life, right?

687
00:26:18.625 --> 00:26:21.971
And I said, "Look, I know you don't do any hobbies now.

688
00:26:21.971 --> 00:26:25.030
Did you ever have a hobby?"

689
00:26:25.030 --> 00:26:26.414
"Well, yeah, I guess,

690
00:26:26.414 --> 00:26:27.312
you know, when I was a kid,

691
00:26:27.312 --> 00:26:28.145
when I was a teenager,

692
00:26:28.145 --> 00:26:29.880
I loved playing with train sets."

693
00:26:29.880 --> 00:26:30.977
I said, "okay, great!

694
00:26:30.977 --> 00:26:33.205
Do you have a train set at home?"

695
00:26:33.205 --> 00:26:34.445
"I've got one at home.

696
00:26:34.445 --> 00:26:36.705
It's in my attic I've not seen it for years."

697
00:26:36.705 --> 00:26:37.945
I said, "Okay, look!

698
00:26:37.945 --> 00:26:40.345
what I would love you to do when you go home tonight

699
00:26:40.345 --> 00:26:42.405
is to get your train set out."

700
00:26:42.405 --> 00:26:44.124
And you got a smirk on your face, right?

701
00:26:44.124 --> 00:26:44.957
(group laughs)

702
00:26:44.957 --> 00:26:45.872
Yeah.

703
00:26:45.872 --> 00:26:46.721
I get it.

704
00:26:46.721 --> 00:26:48.473
Hey, I have no judgment on people's hobbies.

705
00:26:48.473 --> 00:26:50.435
I had a train set when I was a kid.

706
00:26:50.435 --> 00:26:53.287
Well, I thought you were smirking on the fact that

707
00:26:53.287 --> 00:26:55.271
he came in to see me with a problem

708
00:26:55.271 --> 00:26:56.133
he was concerned about,

709
00:26:56.133 --> 00:26:57.804
and my prescription was to get out his train sets.

710
00:26:57.804 --> 00:26:59.394
Oh no, I'm with you on that.

711
00:26:59.394 --> 00:27:00.779
Okay, you're with me on that, okay so.

712
00:27:00.779 --> 00:27:04.779
So he, again, he agrees to go and do this.

713
00:27:04.779 --> 00:27:06.418
So he goes away.

714
00:27:06.418 --> 00:27:08.216
I don't see him then.

715
00:27:08.216 --> 00:27:10.337
It is not uncommon in general practice in the UK for

716
00:27:10.337 --> 00:27:12.661
you not to be able to follow up every single patient,

717
00:27:12.661 --> 00:27:14.956
you just simply cannot do that.

718
00:27:14.956 --> 00:27:16.232
So I got on with my job.

719
00:27:16.232 --> 00:27:18.302
I was, you know, doing my thing.

720
00:27:18.302 --> 00:27:20.306
Three months later,

721
00:27:20.306 --> 00:27:21.722
I finished my morning clinic,

722
00:27:21.722 --> 00:27:24.678
And then we have some go home visits in the UK where,

723
00:27:24.678 --> 00:27:26.339
you know, normally after your morning clinic,

724
00:27:26.339 --> 00:27:28.455
you will then have a list from reception of people

725
00:27:28.455 --> 00:27:30.126
who may be elderly and firm

726
00:27:30.126 --> 00:27:31.315
who can't get out to the practice and

727
00:27:31.315 --> 00:27:33.535
you go and visit them at home to look after them.

728
00:27:33.535 --> 00:27:35.945
So I was, I went to the car park,

729
00:27:35.945 --> 00:27:38.639
to go to do my home visits and I bumped into his wife.

730
00:27:38.639 --> 00:27:40.482
I said, "Hey, hey, look,

731
00:27:40.482 --> 00:27:42.600
how's your husband getting on?"

732
00:27:42.600 --> 00:27:43.436
She said, "Doc Chatterjee,

733
00:27:43.436 --> 00:27:46.323
I feel like I've got the guy I'm married back again.

734
00:27:46.323 --> 00:27:47.701
He comes home from work.

735
00:27:47.701 --> 00:27:49.621
He's straight onto his train set.

736
00:27:49.621 --> 00:27:52.621
He's on eBay all the time, trying to buy collector's items.

737
00:27:52.621 --> 00:27:55.261
And he's subscribed to this

738
00:27:55.261 --> 00:27:56.661
monthly collectors magazine now."

739
00:27:56.661 --> 00:27:57.494
(Rich laughs)

740
00:27:57.494 --> 00:27:58.327
Right?

741
00:27:58.327 --> 00:27:59.173
So she's happy.

742
00:27:59.173 --> 00:28:01.059
He's wearing his conductor hat in house.

743
00:28:01.059 --> 00:28:02.563
Hey but she's happy,

744
00:28:02.563 --> 00:28:04.152
but I still hadn't seen him, right?

745
00:28:04.152 --> 00:28:06.931
Again, roughly three months later,

746
00:28:06.931 --> 00:28:09.301
I'm just going through my clinic as usual.

747
00:28:09.301 --> 00:28:12.408
And I see his name pop up and he has had some blood tests at

748
00:28:12.408 --> 00:28:13.587
a Well Man Check.

749
00:28:13.587 --> 00:28:16.196
And he's coming in to see me to go through them.

750
00:28:16.196 --> 00:28:17.029
Welcoming him.

751
00:28:17.029 --> 00:28:18.294
And before we go into the part of results,

752
00:28:18.294 --> 00:28:20.398
I say, "Hey, look, how are you getting on?"

753
00:28:20.398 --> 00:28:21.866
He says, "Doc, I feel like a different person.

754
00:28:21.866 --> 00:28:23.293
Life is great."

755
00:28:23.293 --> 00:28:24.465
I said, "Mommy's better.

756
00:28:24.465 --> 00:28:25.777
I've got energy.

757
00:28:25.777 --> 00:28:26.610
I said, "Okay, cool.

758
00:28:26.610 --> 00:28:27.944
You know, how's your job?"

759
00:28:27.944 --> 00:28:30.462
"Job, I love it, I love my job now."

760
00:28:30.462 --> 00:28:31.976
"How's your relationship with your wife?"

761
00:28:31.976 --> 00:28:32.834
"Really, really good.

762
00:28:32.834 --> 00:28:34.724
I feel really close to her."

763
00:28:34.724 --> 00:28:36.888
So just like that case,

764
00:28:36.888 --> 00:28:38.843
I don't know how long we've been chatting for

765
00:28:38.843 --> 00:28:39.701
maybe an hour ago.

766
00:28:39.701 --> 00:28:44.368
Just like that's story about the kid who was feeling low

767
00:28:45.911 --> 00:28:49.135
and who had a friendship deficiency,

768
00:28:49.135 --> 00:28:51.328
did this guy have a mental health problem?

769
00:28:51.328 --> 00:28:53.376
Sure, I think he had symptoms that would be consistent.

770
00:28:53.376 --> 00:28:54.395
I could have made,

771
00:28:54.395 --> 00:28:58.145
I could have labeled him and given him a diagnosis, right?

772
00:28:58.145 --> 00:29:02.230
Or did this champ really have a deficiency of passion

773
00:29:02.230 --> 00:29:03.063
in his life?

774
00:29:03.063 --> 00:29:05.179
And the remarkable thing for me is

775
00:29:05.179 --> 00:29:09.029
when that passion deficiency gets corrected.

776
00:29:09.029 --> 00:29:11.029
Not only does he feel better in himself,

777
00:29:11.029 --> 00:29:15.269
but everything else in his life starts to come back online.

778
00:29:15.269 --> 00:29:17.569
The job that he couldn't stand before,

779
00:29:17.569 --> 00:29:18.869
now he enjoys.

780
00:29:18.869 --> 00:29:20.669
The relationship that was a bit tired

781
00:29:20.669 --> 00:29:22.309
and he felt a bit indifferent about,

782
00:29:22.309 --> 00:29:24.509
now he's feeling closer to his wife.

783
00:29:24.509 --> 00:29:26.769
What generally happens is

784
00:29:26.769 --> 00:29:30.769
that individual that came into your office would go into,

785
00:29:30.769 --> 00:29:34.249
would either not seek help or in the event that that person

786
00:29:34.249 --> 00:29:35.911
has the wherewithal to seek help,

787
00:29:35.911 --> 00:29:37.740
would go to a practitioner.

788
00:29:37.740 --> 00:29:40.201
And at least in the United States,

789
00:29:40.201 --> 00:29:43.812
the system is set up wherein that doctor would only have

790
00:29:43.812 --> 00:29:45.777
15 minutes with that person.

791
00:29:45.777 --> 00:29:49.699
And it's a setup rigged to diagnose and prescribe,

792
00:29:49.699 --> 00:29:54.156
and that person would leave with some kind of antidepressant

793
00:29:54.156 --> 00:29:55.827
medication most likely,

794
00:29:55.827 --> 00:30:00.561
and would then consider themselves depressed or clinically,

795
00:30:00.561 --> 00:30:03.295
you know, having some kind of mental malady

796
00:30:03.295 --> 00:30:05.882
without the tactile kind of real world

797
00:30:05.882 --> 00:30:08.375
practical solution that you would implement

798
00:30:08.375 --> 00:30:11.720
because you had the opportunity to provide that person with

799
00:30:11.720 --> 00:30:13.356
a little bit more bandwidth.

800
00:30:13.356 --> 00:30:15.281
I'm so passionate about this Rich,

801
00:30:15.281 --> 00:30:17.877
because it's not as hard as we think it is.

802
00:30:17.877 --> 00:30:19.592
Take small, small steps.

803
00:30:19.592 --> 00:30:21.970
Find one thing you heard us talk about today

804
00:30:21.970 --> 00:30:23.395
that resonates with you,

805
00:30:23.395 --> 00:30:25.026
that you think you want to do.

806
00:30:25.026 --> 00:30:27.190
Don't pick something that you don't wanna do.

807
00:30:27.190 --> 00:30:28.744
Pick something that you do wanna do,

808
00:30:28.744 --> 00:30:31.646
figure out how you can introduce a very small change

809
00:30:31.646 --> 00:30:35.200
in that area consistently for the next few days.

810
00:30:35.200 --> 00:30:36.591
And then re-assess,

811
00:30:36.591 --> 00:30:39.325
and I bet you more often than not,

812
00:30:39.325 --> 00:30:41.120
you gonna feel good about yourself.

813
00:30:41.120 --> 00:30:42.539
Your identity is gonna change

814
00:30:42.539 --> 00:30:44.240
'cause suddenly what happens then,

815
00:30:44.240 --> 00:30:47.925
"Oh, I'm the kind of person who can make behavior change."

816
00:30:47.925 --> 00:30:48.758
Right?

817
00:30:48.758 --> 00:30:50.451
I've done that for the last five days.

818
00:30:50.451 --> 00:30:53.674
That is the approach I have seen over and over again

819
00:30:53.674 --> 00:30:54.812
work for people.

820
00:30:54.812 --> 00:30:58.151
Please if you are struggling, take something from that,

821
00:30:58.151 --> 00:30:59.583
put that into your practice.

822
00:30:59.583 --> 00:31:01.785
Not next Monday, right?

823
00:31:01.785 --> 00:31:03.251
Maybe put it into practice tomorrow.

824
00:31:03.251 --> 00:31:05.676
(bright music)

825
00:31:05.676 --> 00:31:07.296
I absolutely love that advice.

826
00:31:07.296 --> 00:31:08.562
Put it into practice tomorrow.

827
00:31:08.562 --> 00:31:11.428
Start small, start today even,

828
00:31:11.428 --> 00:31:15.081
but does taking action with your mental health always have

829
00:31:15.081 --> 00:31:16.498
to be so literal?

830
00:31:17.478 --> 00:31:20.744
Is it possible that there are circumstances where the best

831
00:31:20.744 --> 00:31:23.825
action you can take is to rest?

832
00:31:23.825 --> 00:31:26.520
Well, that's the perspective of my next guest,

833
00:31:26.520 --> 00:31:27.884
Caroline Burckle.

834
00:31:27.884 --> 00:31:30.217
Caroline is a 2012 Olympian,

835
00:31:31.087 --> 00:31:33.860
a 23 time all American swimmer,

836
00:31:33.860 --> 00:31:37.699
an NCAA champion and an NCAA record holder.

837
00:31:37.699 --> 00:31:38.886
One of my very favorite people.

838
00:31:38.886 --> 00:31:40.513
I absolutely adore her.

839
00:31:40.513 --> 00:31:43.331
She speaks about accepting our emotions,

840
00:31:43.331 --> 00:31:45.498
vulnerability, mentorship,

841
00:31:46.543 --> 00:31:48.039
honest communication,

842
00:31:48.039 --> 00:31:50.706
and the potency latent and rest.

843
00:31:51.625 --> 00:31:53.781
She has an amazing and powerful perspective.

844
00:31:53.781 --> 00:31:55.893
And I can't wait for you to hear it.

845
00:31:55.893 --> 00:31:58.049
(bright music)

846
00:31:58.049 --> 00:31:59.743
Your feelings are valid.

847
00:31:59.743 --> 00:32:00.953
They're not right or wrong.

848
00:32:00.953 --> 00:32:03.248 line:15%
They're not measured,

849
00:32:03.248 --> 00:32:06.379 line:15%
but learning how to use them constructively,

850
00:32:06.379 --> 00:32:08.547 line:15%
whether it's through sharing or understanding it,

851
00:32:08.547 --> 00:32:11.425
communicating and being able to use those and move forward

852
00:32:11.425 --> 00:32:12.370
with whatever that is,

853
00:32:12.370 --> 00:32:13.756
is the key.

854
00:32:13.756 --> 00:32:16.568
So I think we get wrapped up in not saying anything

855
00:32:16.568 --> 00:32:19.101
'cause we're afraid to upset somebody,

856
00:32:19.101 --> 00:32:20.599
but if there's somebody that you can share

857
00:32:20.599 --> 00:32:22.227
how you feel with,

858
00:32:22.227 --> 00:32:24.943
and that feels like a safe space for you,

859
00:32:24.943 --> 00:32:26.894
feels safe to validate your feelings,

860
00:32:26.894 --> 00:32:28.372
say, "It's okay you feel that way.

861
00:32:28.372 --> 00:32:30.073
Let's get a little more information about that."

862
00:32:30.073 --> 00:32:33.018
What is it that's really showing up there and really

863
00:32:33.018 --> 00:32:36.601
understanding that the human's heart first,

864
00:32:37.564 --> 00:32:39.162
they're gonna be able to perform better.

865
00:32:39.162 --> 00:32:41.094
They're gonna be able to take that and use whatever that is

866
00:32:41.094 --> 00:32:43.740
and turn it into something instructive.

867
00:32:43.740 --> 00:32:47.370
Because I think we get stuck in our feelings

868
00:32:47.370 --> 00:32:49.216
and we get stuck just thinking they don't matter,

869
00:32:49.216 --> 00:32:50.935
brushing them away, pushing them away.

870
00:32:50.935 --> 00:32:52.815
Really there's so much power in that.

871
00:32:52.815 --> 00:32:55.323
There's so much information to be had in that.

872
00:32:55.323 --> 00:32:57.438
That's beautiful,

873
00:32:57.438 --> 00:32:59.844
regardless of if they're positive or negative.

874
00:32:59.844 --> 00:33:01.451
You can take that,

875
00:33:01.451 --> 00:33:05.377
remold it and turn it into an amazing performance,

876
00:33:05.377 --> 00:33:08.721
a grade on a test, relationship with somebody,

877
00:33:08.721 --> 00:33:10.877
with your family members,

878
00:33:10.877 --> 00:33:11.710
extracurricular,

879
00:33:11.710 --> 00:33:13.877
whatever it is that that person's wanting to do.

880
00:33:13.877 --> 00:33:16.497
So that's something I like to say,

881
00:33:16.497 --> 00:33:19.661
'cause I think we're shameful of our feelings.

882
00:33:19.661 --> 00:33:21.785
We're shameful of what that means.

883
00:33:21.785 --> 00:33:22.788
There's nothing wrong with that.

884
00:33:22.788 --> 00:33:24.745
It's just information.

885
00:33:24.745 --> 00:33:26.292
And I wish I would've known that.

886
00:33:26.292 --> 00:33:27.293
(Caroline laughs)

887
00:33:27.293 --> 00:33:28.165
I wish I would have been okay

888
00:33:28.165 --> 00:33:29.468
knowing that however I felt was okay.

889
00:33:29.468 --> 00:33:31.260
And that it wasn't,

890
00:33:31.260 --> 00:33:32.714
there's nothing wrong with it.

891
00:33:32.714 --> 00:33:33.808
It was just information.

892
00:33:33.808 --> 00:33:35.767
Yeah, that's beautiful.

893
00:33:35.767 --> 00:33:36.827
I think it's super important.

894
00:33:36.827 --> 00:33:40.127
I feel like at least in the context of sport,

895
00:33:40.127 --> 00:33:42.617
there has been a lot of progress and movement in the right

896
00:33:42.617 --> 00:33:44.651
direction in terms of better understanding

897
00:33:44.651 --> 00:33:46.151
that aspect of it.

898
00:33:47.010 --> 00:33:49.363
I mean, I'm two generations older than you,

899
00:33:49.363 --> 00:33:51.357
but when I see, you know,

900
00:33:51.357 --> 00:33:54.493
the way that young athletes are being coached and mentored

901
00:33:54.493 --> 00:33:57.265
now compared to what it was like when I was a kid, I mean,

902
00:33:57.265 --> 00:33:59.261
that was a long time ago, but you know,

903
00:33:59.261 --> 00:34:00.973
of course, much more,

904
00:34:00.973 --> 00:34:03.012
there's much more to be gleaned and learned.

905
00:34:03.012 --> 00:34:05.261
Totally, mental health is at the forefront right now.

906
00:34:05.261 --> 00:34:08.594
People are caring about how people feel.

907
00:34:11.030 --> 00:34:15.530
And I know this is an important thing to note is that,

908
00:34:16.431 --> 00:34:19.937
you can be struggling and also be okay

909
00:34:19.937 --> 00:34:21.838
with your performances.

910
00:34:21.838 --> 00:34:24.490
You can admit that you're having a hard time

911
00:34:24.490 --> 00:34:27.150
and also you'll still be okay.

912
00:34:27.150 --> 00:34:30.100
You can still be and do great things.

913
00:34:30.100 --> 00:34:31.350
I think there's

914
00:34:34.802 --> 00:34:37.261
a misunderstanding there that if I,

915
00:34:37.261 --> 00:34:38.199
again, back to that,

916
00:34:38.199 --> 00:34:42.699
if I share that I'm depressed or having a hard time or

917
00:34:44.153 --> 00:34:46.113
going through something,

918
00:34:46.113 --> 00:34:48.488
then that means I'm not gonna--

919
00:34:48.488 --> 00:34:51.448
It's gonna impinge on your performance, right?

920
00:34:51.448 --> 00:34:52.588
I mean, that's the whole message

921
00:34:52.588 --> 00:34:53.948
of the weight of gold, right?

922
00:34:53.948 --> 00:34:56.809
It's like kind of a call to action to embrace

923
00:34:56.809 --> 00:34:59.613
that aspect of what it means to be vulnerable

924
00:34:59.613 --> 00:35:01.534
and to have the courage

925
00:35:01.534 --> 00:35:03.661
to raise your hand and ask for help, you know?

926
00:35:03.661 --> 00:35:05.813
The shrouding of it, the hiding of it,

927
00:35:05.813 --> 00:35:08.152
because you're supposed to be,

928
00:35:08.152 --> 00:35:12.313
this Bulletproof individual is not in service to anybody

929
00:35:12.313 --> 00:35:14.298
and certainly not yourself.

930
00:35:14.298 --> 00:35:16.578
And it's not complaining,

931
00:35:16.578 --> 00:35:19.325
it's owning, it's self ownership.

932
00:35:19.325 --> 00:35:22.385
You know, I think there's a difference between complaining.

933
00:35:22.385 --> 00:35:23.665
I have some athletes say,

934
00:35:23.665 --> 00:35:26.717
"Well, I don't wanna complain."

935
00:35:26.717 --> 00:35:29.809
It's if we can shift it to it's self ownership,

936
00:35:29.809 --> 00:35:32.038
then we can take that and use it,

937
00:35:32.038 --> 00:35:33.938
just because you're having a hard time

938
00:35:33.938 --> 00:35:35.468
have asked for help, et cetera,

939
00:35:35.468 --> 00:35:38.018
doesn't mean you're all of a sudden gonna poorly.

940
00:35:38.018 --> 00:35:40.858
It doesn't mean that you're not focusing on your swimming

941
00:35:40.858 --> 00:35:42.532
or your baseball or whatever.

942
00:35:42.532 --> 00:35:45.182
It means that you're doing something for your mental health,

943
00:35:45.182 --> 00:35:46.935
which will then help that.

944
00:35:46.935 --> 00:35:48.891
So it's a perspective shift.

945
00:35:48.891 --> 00:35:50.399
Right, it does require some level

946
00:35:50.399 --> 00:35:53.423
of mind-body integration though,

947
00:35:53.423 --> 00:35:57.212
because you have to know when you are copping out like,

948
00:35:57.212 --> 00:35:58.949
am I just wimping out here?

949
00:35:58.949 --> 00:36:03.868
Or am I really in jeopardy where I need some outside help?

950
00:36:03.868 --> 00:36:04.928
Like there is a difference.

951
00:36:04.928 --> 00:36:07.908
And if you don't have that kind of intuition,

952
00:36:07.908 --> 00:36:09.136
then you know,

953
00:36:09.136 --> 00:36:11.296
you might be just, you know,

954
00:36:11.296 --> 00:36:13.416
indulging in your laziness,

955
00:36:13.416 --> 00:36:16.642
you know, instead of really being in peril.

956
00:36:16.642 --> 00:36:20.480
But that's where great coaches and mentors come in,

957
00:36:20.480 --> 00:36:22.188
because if somebody is vulnerable enough

958
00:36:22.188 --> 00:36:23.897
to share whatever it is,

959
00:36:23.897 --> 00:36:27.418
then they can catch that and be able to change that and turn

960
00:36:27.418 --> 00:36:28.839
that into something.

961
00:36:28.839 --> 00:36:32.155
Otherwise, they will just sit in their shit (chuckles)

962
00:36:32.155 --> 00:36:32.988
so to speak

963
00:36:32.988 --> 00:36:35.634
and, you know, let it go to shit and,

964
00:36:35.634 --> 00:36:37.434
"well, whatever, I'm just copping out,"

965
00:36:37.434 --> 00:36:38.267
You know?

966
00:36:38.267 --> 00:36:40.373
So if they can articulate that,

967
00:36:40.373 --> 00:36:42.693
communicate that information to whomever,

968
00:36:42.693 --> 00:36:44.212
coach, mentor.

969
00:36:44.212 --> 00:36:47.132
And this is where coaches and mentors can hear that,

970
00:36:47.132 --> 00:36:50.032
taking that information and being able to help somebody,

971
00:36:50.032 --> 00:36:52.918
being able to identify it without shame

972
00:36:52.918 --> 00:36:54.822
and turn it into something better,

973
00:36:54.822 --> 00:36:57.523
call them out on what it is in a nice way,

974
00:36:57.523 --> 00:36:58.356
in a beautiful way,

975
00:36:58.356 --> 00:37:00.015
like, "Hey, you're tough.

976
00:37:00.015 --> 00:37:00.855
Let's make something out of this.

977
00:37:00.855 --> 00:37:02.955
Like we're not gonna let this happen to you here."

978
00:37:02.955 --> 00:37:06.294
That's where things can shift for the positive.

979
00:37:06.294 --> 00:37:08.121
This is applicable outside of sport,

980
00:37:08.121 --> 00:37:09.265
in the workplace,

981
00:37:09.265 --> 00:37:12.383
like a boss-employee relationship,

982
00:37:12.383 --> 00:37:14.210
or how management is structured so that

983
00:37:14.210 --> 00:37:18.378
the work staff feels like they can raise certain issues.

984
00:37:18.378 --> 00:37:20.268
And the manager is creating an environment

985
00:37:20.268 --> 00:37:21.943
that's conducive to that.

986
00:37:21.943 --> 00:37:25.492
I think ultimately leads to a much more effective,

987
00:37:25.492 --> 00:37:27.575
healthy place to work or,

988
00:37:28.413 --> 00:37:30.918
environment to excel as an athlete.

989
00:37:30.918 --> 00:37:31.795
Absolutely.

990
00:37:31.795 --> 00:37:34.561
And that is one of the most important things that I think

991
00:37:34.561 --> 00:37:36.228
any organization can hold.

992
00:37:36.228 --> 00:37:38.061
I mean, coaches, teams,

993
00:37:38.061 --> 00:37:38.894
USA swimming,

994
00:37:38.894 --> 00:37:40.941
is that if the standard is held there and if

995
00:37:40.941 --> 00:37:45.709
people are all believing in whatever leadership there is,

996
00:37:45.709 --> 00:37:47.746
and the leaders are able to develop these people,

997
00:37:47.746 --> 00:37:51.090
athletes, employees, whatever it is,

998
00:37:51.090 --> 00:37:55.428
and to a space of vulnerability openness and action

999
00:37:55.428 --> 00:37:57.012
on what it is that we're doing,

1000
00:37:57.012 --> 00:37:58.555
not just talking about it and sitting around,

1001
00:37:58.555 --> 00:38:00.433
it's like actually acting on it.

1002
00:38:00.433 --> 00:38:01.347
Whether that action is,

1003
00:38:01.347 --> 00:38:02.887
"Let's sit with this for a minute."

1004
00:38:02.887 --> 00:38:03.720
Or that action is,

1005
00:38:03.720 --> 00:38:05.682
"Let's take action and do something right now."

1006
00:38:05.682 --> 00:38:08.685
Both of those things are beautiful.

1007
00:38:08.685 --> 00:38:10.942
That's how things develop.

1008
00:38:10.942 --> 00:38:15.565
And yeah, those are my greatest learnings from Mike DuBois.

1009
00:38:15.565 --> 00:38:17.526
He was just very much either like,

1010
00:38:17.526 --> 00:38:21.323
"Okay, let's now that we have that information,

1011
00:38:21.323 --> 00:38:24.185
let's either go this way and act on it immediately.

1012
00:38:24.185 --> 00:38:26.242
Or I'm gonna let you sit with that for a second.

1013
00:38:26.242 --> 00:38:28.195
I'm here right here next to you,

1014
00:38:28.195 --> 00:38:30.085
but I'm gonna let you sit with that for a second."

1015
00:38:30.085 --> 00:38:32.794
And like, knowing that I was like held in that space,

1016
00:38:32.794 --> 00:38:34.102
I think reflecting back,

1017
00:38:34.102 --> 00:38:35.199
that makes all the difference.

1018
00:38:35.199 --> 00:38:37.644
'Cause either ways an action.

1019
00:38:37.644 --> 00:38:38.986
You're empowering them either way.

1020
00:38:38.986 --> 00:38:41.178
They gotta figure it out on their own either way,

1021
00:38:41.178 --> 00:38:43.079
but it's the person that's gonna hold steady

1022
00:38:43.079 --> 00:38:45.515
and sturdy there that's holding a leadership standard.

1023
00:38:45.515 --> 00:38:47.384
(bright music)

1024
00:38:47.384 --> 00:38:49.190
This next segment is from one

1025
00:38:49.190 --> 00:38:51.468
of the most influential people in my life.

